In this lesson, we'll look at arrangements, which is a fancy word for orderings where the order really matters. Imagine four friends lining up for a photo. If they swap places, that counts as a brand-new lineup. So the big idea is, whenever changing the order gives you a different outcome, you're dealing with an arrangement. First up, arranging everything. If you've got n distinct objects and you want to line them all up in a row, the number of ways to do it is n factorial, which just means you multiply n by every whole number counting down to one. So for four people, that's four times three times two times one, which gives you twenty-four lineups. Next, arranging some of them. Sometimes you don't use everything. If you only pick r objects out of n, you still multiply a descending list, but this time it stops once you've picked r of them. And a quick convention to remember, zero factorial equals one, which sounds weird but makes the maths work out neatly. Now let's see it in action.